## JWT Expiry Check Bug

The most common cause is a **units mismatch**. JWT `exp` is in **seconds**, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**.

---

### The Bug

```javascript
// ❌ BROKEN - comparing milliseconds to seconds
const isExpired = Date.now() > payload.exp;

// Example: Date.now() = 1700000000000 (milliseconds)
// payload.exp = 1700000100        (seconds)
// 1700000000000 > 1700000100 is ALWAYS true... 
// wait, that means it should always reject?

// Actually the opposite bug is more common:
const isExpired = Date.now() < payload.exp; // wrong operator
// OR the check is inverted, letting everything through:
if (!isExpired) {
  return next(); // proceeds even when expired
}
```

The **real subtle bug** — comparing wrong units means the condition is never what you expect:

```javascript
// ❌ This ALWAYS passes (never blocks expired tokens)
// Date.now() = 1,700,000,000,000 ms
// exp        =     1,700,000,100 sec
// Date.now() is ALWAYS greater, so this logic breaks completely
if (Date.now() > payload.exp) {
  // This always fires, even for valid tokens
  return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Expired' });
}
```

---

### The Fix

```javascript
// ✅ Convert exp to milliseconds before comparing
const isExpired = Date.now() > payload.exp * 1000;

// Or convert Date.now() to seconds
const nowInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
const isExpired = nowInSeconds > payload.exp;
```

---

### Complete Corrected Middleware

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function authMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;

  if (!authHeader?.startsWith('Bearer ')) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Missing or malformed token' });
  }

  const token = authHeader.split(' ')[1];

  try {
    // Option A: Let jsonwebtoken handle expiry (RECOMMENDED)
    // It checks exp automatically and throws TokenExpiredError
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    req.user = payload;
    return next();

  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'TokenExpiredError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
    }
    if (err.name === 'JsonWebTokenError') {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
    }
    return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Auth error' });
  }
}

// Option B: Manual check if you're decoding without verifying
function manualExpiryCheck(payload) {
  const nowInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);

  if (!payload.exp) {
    throw new Error('Token has no expiry');   // reject tokens without exp
  }

  if (nowInSeconds > payload.exp) {
    throw new Error('Token expired');
  }
}
```

---

### Why `jwt.verify()` Is Preferred

| Approach | Handles exp? | Handles signature? | Safe? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `jwt.verify()` | ✅ Auto | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |
| `jwt.decode()` + manual check | ⚠️ Manual | ❌ No | ❌ Dangerous |
| Manual decode + wrong units | ❌ Broken | ❌ No | ❌ No |

**`jwt.decode()` does not verify the signature** — an attacker can forge any payload. Always use `jwt.verify()` with your secret/public key.

---

### Quick Diagnostic

```javascript
// Run this to see what your token actually contains
const decoded = jwt.decode(token);
console.log({
  exp: decoded.exp,                          // e.g. 1700000100
  expAsDate: new Date(decoded.exp * 1000),   // human readable
  now: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000),        // current time in seconds
  isExpired: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) > decoded.exp
});
```

The fix is almost always either the **`* 1000` unit conversion** or switching to `jwt.verify()` and letting the library handle it correctly.
